{
  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "agent_resolution_discrepancy (resolution time)",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "assigned_agent",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "The query asks why some AGENTS have longer resolution times; the agent identity is the candidate cause whose effect on resolution time is being attributed. It is explicitly named in the query."
    },
    {
      "name": "incident_category",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Category (e.g., email_server vs vpn) plausibly drives which agents get assigned (routing/specialization) and independently affects resolution time. A genuine common cause of both treatment and outcome, surfaced from data, not named in query."
    },
    {
      "name": "issue_complexity",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Complexity can influence assignment (harder tickets routed to certain agents) and directly lengthens resolution time. A common cause of both; not named in query."
    },
    {
      "name": "incident_severity_proxy",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Severity/impact affects who is assigned (escalation to senior agents) and the effort/time to resolve. Plausible common cause of treatment and outcome; not named in query."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolution_mechanism",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The required fix (server_restart, credentials_reset, etc.) lies on the causal path between the agent's handling/incident and the resolution time; it is a consequence of how the ticket is worked, so it is a mediator, not a confounder. Adjusting for it would be a mistake."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["assigned_agent"],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "incident_category", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.8},
    {"name": "issue_complexity", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.85},
    {"name": "incident_severity_proxy", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.8}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.8,
  "rationale": "The columns set up a genuine causal estimation: assigned_agent is the treatment (named in query), and the outcome is resolution time. Three confounders (category, complexity, severity_proxy) are plausible common causes of both agent assignment and resolution time, and none were named in the query, demonstrating real surfacing of confounders. resolution_mechanism is a mediator, not a confounder, so it should not be adjusted for. Overall this is a strong instance of causal attribution with novel confounders, slightly discounted for including a mediator alongside the confounders."
}

